Exact quantiles of tau: 5% 0.246, median 2.749, 95% 9.842.
Log Bayes factor, hierarchical model against complete pooling: -0.467 (Bayes factor 0.63).
\image(figures/bayes_shrinkage.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the exact posterior density of \(\tau\) (black) against histograms of the non-centred (blue bars) and centred (orange line) draws. Right: each school's raw estimate (red), its posterior mean (blue, with a white dot at the exact mean) and 90% interval; the grey line is the posterior mean of \(\mu\).)
\alttext(Left: posterior density of tau from 0 to 16. A black exact curve falls from 0.2 at tau 0 to near 0 at 15; blue non-centred bars follow it closely; an orange centred line starts at only 0.12 in the first bin, then runs above the black curve between about 1 and 9. Right: for schools A to H, red raw estimates at 28, 8, -3, 7, -1, 1, 18, 12 and blue posterior means between 3.5 and 6.1, with 90% intervals of about ±8 points, all crossing a grey line at 4.4.)

Every non-centred posterior mean is within 1.7 Monte Carlo standard errors of the exact value,
with \(\hat R\le1.005\) and at least 1,000 effective draws per parameter. The errors are all
slightly negative because every \(\theta_j\) depends on \(\mu\), whose estimate is 0.10 low. The
histogram of \(\tau\) follows the exact density, while the centred draws have too little mass
near zero and too much between 1 and 9.

The data barely constrain \(\tau\). Its posterior median is 2.75, about half the prior median of 5,
but the 90% interval runs from 0.25 to 9.84. The exact log evidence
gives a Bayes factor of 0.63 for the hierarchical model against complete pooling. That is mild
support for pooling, consistent with the homogeneity test of §2. Because \(\tau\) is probably
small next to the standard errors, the average shrinkage factor is between 0.84 and 0.94.
Each school's estimate moves most of the way to the common mean: school A's 28 points becomes
about 6 (90% interval \(-1.6\) to 16.0), and school C's \(-3\) becomes 3.9. The least-shrunk
schools are E, B and G, which have the smallest standard errors (9 and 10 points). The
hierarchical model therefore reports one modest common effect of about 4 points with wide
uncertainty, and no school whose coaching stands out.

> Summary

- A sampler should be checked against an exact answer where possible. The conjugate pooled
  model (`bayes::normal_mean_posterior`) and the quadrature of the hierarchical model
  (`bayes::hierarchical_normal_posterior`) gave exact targets, and MAP, HMC and NUTS matched them
  to within Monte Carlo error.
- HMC preserves the posterior because Hamiltonian flow conserves energy and volume and is
  reversible. Leapfrog keeps the last two exactly, and a Metropolis test corrects the energy
  error. On a Gaussian a leapfrog step is a linear map with trace \(2-h^2\), stable only for
  \(\varepsilon<2s\), and the expected acceptance (`bayes::leapfrog_gaussian_acceptance`) matched
  simulation to within 0.01.
- The hierarchical eight-schools model has no posterior mode, and its centred form is a funnel
  whose neck needs steps below about \(2\tau\). There NUTS reported 100 divergences and an E-BFMI
  of 0.15, and its draws of \(\tau\) were biased upward. Non-centring \(\theta_j=\mu+\tau\eta_j\)
  removed the problem and cut the cost per effective draw of \(\log\tau\) by a factor of about 70.
- Per-draw divergences, tree depths and energies (`NUTSResult`) and multi-chain ESS,
  \(\hat R\) and E-BFMI (`bayes::summarize_draws`, `bayes::energy_bfmi`) are the diagnostics that
  exposed the failure. \(\hat R\) alone did not.
- Substantively, the eight schools are consistent with one small common coaching effect
  (posterior mean of \(\mu\) 4.4 points, sd 3.3), and every school's estimate is shrunk most of the way towards it.

> References

- Betancourt, M. (2017). A conceptual introduction to Hamiltonian Monte Carlo. arXiv:1701.02434.
- Gelman, A., Carlin, J. B., Stern, H. S., Dunson, D. B., Vehtari, A. and Rubin, D. B. (2013).
  *Bayesian Data Analysis*, 3rd edition. Chapman and Hall/CRC. §5.5 (eight schools), §11.4–11.5
  (\(\hat R\) and effective sample size).
- Hoffman, M. D. and Gelman, A. (2014). The No-U-Turn Sampler: adaptively setting path lengths in
  Hamiltonian Monte Carlo. *Journal of Machine Learning Research* 15, 1593–1623.
- Neal, R. M. (2011). MCMC using Hamiltonian dynamics. In *Handbook of Markov Chain Monte Carlo*,
  ch. 5. Chapman and Hall/CRC.
- Rubin, D. B. (1981). Estimation in parallel randomized experiments. *Journal of Educational
  Statistics* 6, 377–401.
